      "writtenOn": "2026-10-14T15:25:42Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "quote data model spec version 2026.2\n\n```\nThe link to the environment metrics for this chassis.\n•\nEnvironmentMetrics (v1.15+) {\nThis property shall contain a link to a resource of type\nEnvironmentMetrics that specifies the environment metrics for this\nchassis, all containing chassis, and devices contained by any of these\nchassis instances. When determining power and energy readings, care\nshould be taken to ensure any reported values do not overlap or result\nin double-counting.\nobject\nSee the EnvironmentMetrics schema for details on this property.\n```\n\nIt sounds very much like a property that is modeled after resource containment rather than `cooled_by`.\n\nSaw this response on redfishforum.com https://redfishforum.com/post/4062/thread\n\n\u003e More generically speaking, since \"Chassis\" is a container with some well-known physical boundary (perhaps it\u0027s the sheet metal of the box), the context of \"EnvironmentMetrics\" would contain everything inside of said boundary. So, if your had a Chassis resource that represents an entire rack, the PowerWatts property inside of its EnvironmentMetrics resource would represent the power of that entire rack (including any nested Chassis resources referenced by the \"Contains\" property).\n\nso assuming the previous code was consistent (modeling subordinate resource via `cooled_by`) then `containing` should be the right adaptation for my change with the option enabled?\n\nplease let me know if your spec interpretation is different here",
      "parentUuid": "c5c934e9_ee53649b",
      "revId": "7260e862c3cfb357979c192dfb10f9ffe78c9ab2",
      "serverId": "adbd0a64-1f21-4d83-b585-671fe73cb6e4"
    },
